Full PXE network boot test (DHCP -> TFTP -> NBD -> ldm)
============================================================

What this covers: an end-to-end test of the actual client boot chain a
real thin client goes through - DHCP handing out a boot file, TFTP
serving the bootloader/kernel/initrd, the initrd connecting to an NBD
server for the root filesystem, and switch_root handing off into the
real ltsp6 client system - as opposed to the earlier ldm/xrdp testing
in this repo's other doc files, which ran ldm directly on an
already-booted host and proved the greeter/xfreerdp/xrdp chain works,
but not the network-boot chain itself.

Test setup: an isolated Hyper-V "Internal" switch (no other traffic on
it) plus, later, a parallel comparison client on pure Proxmox VE/QEMU
(direct kernel boot via the VM's "args:" config line, bypassing PXE
entirely) to isolate hypervisor-specific issues from real bugs. Both
used the same kernel/initrd/squashfs client image, a small Debian 13
VM running dnsmasq (DHCP+TFTP in one) and nbd-server.

This test surfaced a chain of real, independent bugs, each hiding
behind the previous one - fixing one just exposed the next failure
further down the boot sequence. All of them are now understood and
fixed. The end state, confirmed by live testing: Xorg actually starts
and runs (`/usr/lib/xorg/Xorg :7 vt7 ...`) as a real process on the
client.


Bug 1: nbdroot= comma vs colon syntax (fixed earlier, see git history)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
initramfs-tools' stock nbd script (/usr/share/initramfs-tools/scripts/
local-top/nbd) parses nbdroot= differently depending on the separator:
a 2-field "server,port" (SIMPLE case, no export name support) vs the
default "server:port/exportname" case. Using a comma
(nbdroot=host,port/export) matches the wrong branch and silently fails
NBD setup with an empty export name, which looks like an early boot
hang on the console (the retry loop in /scripts/local-block doesn't
print anything new). Fix: use the colon form,
nbdroot=<server>:<port>/<export>, matching initramfs-tools' own default
case and every real-world ltsp5-uefi/ltsp.ipxe example.


Bug 2: init=/usr/sbin/init-ltsp vs init=/sbin/init-ltsp
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
client/Debian/share/initramfs-tools/scripts/init-bottom/ltsp (the hook
that mounts a writable tmpfs overlay over the read-only squashfs root
before handing off to userspace) and .../init-premount/udhcp (LTSP's
own DHCP client, see bug 3) both gate their entire body on:

    grep -qs "init=/sbin/init-ltsp" /proc/cmdline || exit 0

A kernel cmdline of init=/usr/sbin/init-ltsp (note /usr/sbin) does NOT
match this substring check, so both hooks silently no-op. Without the
overlay, root stays read-only: systemd-logind fails with
"Failed to set up special execution directory in /var/lib: Read-only
file system" (exit 238/STATE_DIRECTORY), and ldm fails immediately with
"Couldn't open logfile /var/log/ldm.log" - both are the same underlying
cause, not two separate bugs, and neither is related to ldm's own
systemd registration (see the "ldm never started at boot" fix below -
that fix is still correct and necessary, just not sufficient by
itself). Fix: use init=/sbin/init-ltsp on the kernel cmdline, exactly
as documented at the top of the udhcp script.


Bug 3: udhcp script passes a bogus "-r dhcp" to its own DHCP client
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
Once bug 2 is fixed, LTSP's own init-premount/udhcp hook starts
running (previously silently skipped) and immediately loops forever
printing "DHCP request for <iface>..." without ever obtaining a lease -
reproduced identically on both Hyper-V (dnsmasq) and a pure Proxmox/
QEMU comparison VM, ruling out a hypervisor- or DHCP-server-specific
cause. Root cause, found via the script's own `xtrace=udhcp` kernel
cmdline debug switch: initramfs-tools' own cmdline parsing pre-sets a
shell variable $ip to the raw value of the kernel's ip= parameter
(here, the literal string "dhcp", from `ip=dhcp`). The udhcp script's
process_kernel_parameters() correctly recognizes this "ip=dhcp"
shorthand and sets autoconf="dhcp", but never clears the pre-existing
$ip variable - so do_dhcp() later builds `-r $ip`, i.e. an actual
`udhcpc -r dhcp` invocation, asking to request the literal (invalid) IP
address "dhcp". Fix (client/Debian/share/initramfs-tools/scripts/
init-premount/udhcp): explicitly `unset ip tftp router subnet hostname`
in the shorthand-keyword case of process_kernel_parameters().


Bug 4: ldm segfaults (NULL pointer) when the greeter process dies
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
With bugs 2 and 3 fixed, ldm.service starts, systemd-logind works, and
ldm itself runs - and then reliably segfaults a moment later. Root
cause, found via a debug build under gdb (reproduced identically by
running /usr/sbin/ldm directly): ldmgreetercomm.c's listen_greeter():

    GError *ge = NULL;
    if (g_io_channel_read_line(greeterr, ..., &ge) != G_IO_STATUS_NORMAL) {
        log_entry("ldm", 3, "%s", ge->message);   /* ge can be NULL here */

g_io_channel_read_line() only populates *error on G_IO_STATUS_ERROR;
on G_IO_STATUS_EOF (the greeter process died and closed its end of the
pipe) it leaves ge as NULL, and the unconditional ge->message
dereference (a GError's `message` field sits at byte offset 8) crashes
with a NULL+8 fault. Fix (ldm-mabutz, src/ldmgreetercomm.c): only
dereference ge when it's non-NULL, and log a distinct "channel closed
unexpectedly" message on EOF instead. This is a correctness fix
regardless of what caused the greeter to die - see bugs 5-8 for what
was actually killing it in this test.


Bugs 5-8: nothing ever started Xorg
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
The greeter (ldmgtkgreet) was dying immediately with "Gtk-WARNING:
cannot open display: :0" because no X server was ever running. Several
compounding gaps, found and fixed together:

  - ldm.service's ExecStart ran /usr/sbin/ldm directly. That's wrong:
    ldm itself is only the X *client* (display manager); starting the
    X *server* is screen.d/ldm's job, which does
    `exec xinit /usr/share/ltsp/xinitrc /usr/sbin/ldm -- ${DISPLAY}
    vt${TTY} ...`, and screen.d/ldm is only ever reached via
    /usr/share/ltsp/screen_session <N>. Fixed (ldm-mabutz,
    debian/ldm.service): ExecStart=/usr/share/ltsp/screen_session 07
    (07 is the default SCREEN_07=ldm mapping), with TTYPath/
    UtmpIdentifier/Conflicts updated from tty1 to tty7 (the classic
    graphical-VT convention screen.d/ldm's `vt${TTY}` argument assumes).

  - screen_session and screen.d/ldm call shell functions
    (boolean_is_true, run_parts_list, die, ...) that are supposed to
    come from /usr/share/ltsp/ltsp-client-common-functions, sourced via
    ltsp-client-functions. That file was never being installed for the
    ltsp-client-core package at all: debian/rules has the install line
    for the ltsp-server package (`install common/ltsp-common-functions
    .../ltsp-server-common-functions`) but the equivalent line for
    ltsp-client-core was simply missing. Without it, every call to
    those functions fails with "not found", and screen_session's own
    script-resolution logic breaks down entirely (observed as
    "/usr/share/ltsp/screen.d/: Permission denied" - the empty,
    unresolved script variable ends up pointing at the directory
    itself). Fixed: added the missing install line to debian/rules.

  - Two runtime dependencies screen_session/screen.d/ldm actually shell
    out to were missing from ltsp-client-core's package dependencies:
    fgconsole (package kbd) and lspci (package pciutils, used by the
    XS20-*-hack screen-session.d scripts). Fixed: added kbd and
    pciutils to debian/control's Depends.

  - xinit itself (the package providing /usr/bin/xinit) was missing
    too - screen.d/ldm's `exec xinit ...` line silently failed with
    "No such file or directory", swallowed by the script's own
    `>/dev/null 2>&1` redirect, which is why this had to be tracked
    down by re-running the exact same xinit invocation manually with
    visible output rather than relying on the journal. Fixed: added
    xinit to debian/control's Depends.

  - screen_session's own fgconsole-polling loop
    (`while ... [ $(fgconsole) -ne "$SCREEN_NUM" ]; do sleep 2; done`)
    waits for VT7 to already be the foreground console before ever
    running screen.d/ldm - a leftover assumption from the old inittab/
    sysvinit multi-getty model, where some other screen would actively
    switch to it. Under a single ldm.service unit with nothing else
    ever switching VTs, that wait never ends: Xorg itself performs the
    VT7 switch (via the `vt7` argument screen.d/ldm passes it), but
    Xorg never gets to run in the first place. Fixed
    (ltsp_config.d/15default-screen): default
    SCREEN_07_WAIT_FGCONSOLE=False alongside SCREEN_07=ldm, so ldm's
    screen starts immediately and lets Xorg claim VT7 itself.

Verified end to end after all of the above: `ps aux` on the booted
client shows both `xinit /usr/share/ltsp/xinitrc /usr/sbin/ldm -- :7
vt7 ...` and its child `/usr/lib/xorg/Xorg :7 vt7 ... -depth 16 -br`
actually running.


What worked completely: the network boot chain itself
-------------------------------------------------------
Both of the two standard PXE boot paths correctly complete the full
DHCP -> TFTP -> NBD handshake (after fixing an unrelated host-routing
issue - see below):

  - BIOS/legacy path (pxelinux): dnsmasq's dhcp-boot serving
    pxelinux.0 directly, a pxelinux.cfg/default with the kernel/initrd
    and a `nbdroot=<server>:<port>/<export>` append line (see bug 1
    above for why the colon, not comma, form is required).

  - UEFI path (iPXE, the approach used by the ltsp5-uefi project -
    https://github.com/alkisg/ltsp5-uefi): dnsmasq tags the request
    with dhcp-userclass=set:ipxe,iPXE, serving Debian's prebuilt
    /usr/lib/ipxe/ipxe.efi on the client's first (non-iPXE) DHCP
    request, then a plain iPXE script (kernel/initrd/boot commands,
    same nbdroot= syntax) on iPXE's own second DHCP request once it
    identifies itself. Since this test only needed amd64 (not a 32-bit
    OS on a UEFI client), the simple direct kernel/initrd/boot path
    was enough - no grub chainload workaround needed (that workaround,
    used by ltsp5-uefi, is specifically for booting a 32-bit OS on a
    UEFI client, which iPXE itself can't do).

In both cases, dnsmasq's logs showed the complete expected sequence
(DHCPDISCOVER/OFFER/REQUEST/ACK, each boot file served over TFTP in
order, then a second DHCP round from inside the kernel's own ip=dhcp
network setup - or LTSP's own udhcp client once bug 2/3 were fixed)
and netstat on the server confirmed a real, established TCP connection
to nbd-server's port.


Real bug found and fixed: a rogue DHCP-issued default route
-------------------------------------------------------------------
While setting this up, dnsmasq's DHCP handed out a default gateway
option pointing at itself, and the Windows host itself picked up that
lease on its own virtual adapter for the isolated test switch - which
briefly overrode the host's real default route (0.0.0.0/0) with one
pointing into the isolated test network, cutting off ALL internet
access from both the host and WSL2 until noticed and fixed. Two
independent fixes, both worth doing on a real deployment's boot/PXE
server, not just this test: explicitly suppress the router and DNS
DHCP options with empty `dhcp-option=3` / `dhcp-option=6` directives
(clients only need to reach the boot server, not get routed to the
internet through it), and keep DHCP/PXE infrastructure on a switch/VLAN
that nothing else - especially not the management host - has an
interface on.


Previously reported "unresolved Hyper-V-specific early-boot hang":
superseded - it was bug 1 above
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
An earlier version of this document described a seemingly
Hyper-V-specific, non-deterministic early-boot freeze (~0.2s kernel
uptime, moving between runs, five different workarounds tried and
ruled out: efi=noruntime, removing the Generation-2 SCSI controller,
blacklisting hv_balloon, a genuinely single-vCPU VM, ipv6.disable=1).
That entire investigation was chasing a red herring: the actual cause
was bug 1 (the nbdroot= comma-vs-colon parsing bug), whose failure mode
- an infinite silent retry loop in /scripts/local-block - produces a
static console with no new output, which is indistinguishable from a
genuine hang unless you specifically capture and diff full boot logs
across multiple attempts or get a real serial console with the actual
error message on it. Once a proper serial console capture
(console=ttyS0,115200n8 plus a real Hyper-V COM port / QEMU serial
socket, not just the graphical console) was used, the actual
"Insufficient information to set up nbd, quitting" failure message was
immediately visible, and none of the five Hyper-V-specific workarounds
above were ever actually necessary. They're recorded here only so
nobody re-tries them expecting a different result.
